Command	                                                                                Use

date	                                             Displays the current system date and time.
w	                                                 Displays information about currently logged-in users and their system activity.
date > os.txt	                                     Redirects the output of the date command into os.txt, overwriting its contents.
w >> os.txt	                                       Appends the output of the w command to os.txt.
cat os.txt	                                       Displays the contents of os.txt.
cat > Names.txt	                                   Creates Names.txt and allows the user to enter text into it.
sed -n '$p' Names.txt	                             Displays only the last line of Names.txt.
sed -n '2p' Names.txt	                             Displays only the second line of Names.txt.
sed '1d' Names.txt	                               Deletes the first line from the displayed output.
sed -n '2,6p' Names.txt	                           Displays lines 2 to 6 of Names.txt.
sed 's/Sharma/Verma/' Names.txt	                   Replaces the first occurrence of Sharma with Verma on each line.
sed '2s/Sharma/Verma/' Names.txt	                 Replaces Sharma with Verma only in the second line.
sed -n 'p' Names.txt	                             Prints all lines of Names.txt.
sed 's/[^ ]*/XYZ/' Names.txt	                     Replaces the first sequence of non-space characters in each line with XYZ.
sed 's/\([^ ]*\)/{\1}/' Names.txt	                 Encloses the first word of each line in curly braces.
sed 's/\([^ ]*\)/\1/' Names.txt	                   Matches the first word and replaces it with itself (no visible change).
sed 's/^\([^ ]*\) \([^ ]*\)/\2 \1/' Names.txt	     Swaps the first and second words of each line.
